<div class="hero">
  <div class="badge">Beagle Cost Guide 2025</div>
  <h1>How much does a Beagle <em>really</em> cost?</h1>
  <p>Beagles are one of the most affordable breeds to own — but their nose gets them into expensive trouble. Get the real numbers before you bring one home.</p>
  <div class="hero-stats">
    <div class="hstat"><span class="n">$800+</span><span class="l">Average purchase price</span></div>
    <div class="hstat"><span class="n">$2,800</span><span class="l">First year total cost</span></div>
    <div class="hstat"><span class="n">$18k+</span><span class="l">Lifetime cost estimate</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="ad-top"><div class="ad-zone">Advertisement - Google AdSense 728x90</div></div>
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">RealPetCosts</a> &rsaquo; Dog Breeds &rsaquo; Beagle</div>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <div class="warning-box">
        <h3>Beagles follow their nose — straight into vet bills</h3>
        <p>Beagles are scent hounds bred to track and follow smells relentlessly. They will escape any yard, eat anything they find, and howl for hours if bored. Budget for a 6-foot fence ($2,000-$5,000 one-time), professional training ($400-$800), and the inevitable vet visit when they eat something they shouldn't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-card">
        <div class="calc-head">
          <h2>Beagle cost calculator</h2>
          <p>2025 cost data — one of the most affordable breeds to own</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-body">
          <div class="ins-banner">
            <h4>Pet insurance is a good idea for Beagles</h4>
            <p>Beagles are curious eaters — foreign body ingestion is common. Epilepsy and hip dysplasia also affect the breed. Insurance at $35-$60/month provides solid protection for this adventurous breed.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="flbl">View costs</span>
          <div class="vtabs">
            <button class="vt on" id="tab-first">First year</button>
            <button class="vt" id="tab-ongoing">Ongoing / year</button>
          </div>
          <div class="sgrid">
            <div class="sc hi"><div class="sl" id="c1l">First year total</div><div class="sv" id="c1v">$2,800</div></div>
            <div class="sc"><div class="sl">Lifetime (13 yrs)</div><div class="sv" id="c2v">$18k</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bklist" id="breakdown"></div>
          <div class="mobox"><span class="ml">Monthly budget to set aside</span><span class="mv" id="monthly">$233/mo</span></div>
          <p class="fine">Based on 2025 Rover, ASPCA, and breed health data. Does not include purchase price ($500-$1,500) or fencing costs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="section-card">
        <h2>Beagle health costs to expect</h2>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:#4A4540;margin-bottom:16px;line-height:1.6">Beagles are generally one of the healthier breeds but their curiosity and food obsession lead to preventable vet visits. Budget for the unexpected.</p>
        <div class="health-grid">
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Epilepsy</div><div class="hi-cost">$200 - $600/yr</div><div class="hi-desc">Idiopathic epilepsy is more common in Beagles than many breeds. Requires lifelong anticonvulsant medication and regular vet monitoring.</div></div>
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Foreign Body Ingestion</div><div class="hi-cost">$1,500 - $4,000</div><div class="hi-desc">Beagles eat first, think later. Intestinal obstruction surgery is a real risk. Baby-proof your home and yard. Secure all trash and food items.</div></div>
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Hip Dysplasia</div><div class="hi-cost">$1,500 - $4,000</div><div class="hi-desc">Less common than in larger breeds but still occurs. Surgery or pain management required in affected dogs.</div></div>
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Ear Infections</div><div class="hi-cost">$150 - $400/yr</div><div class="hi-desc">Long floppy ears trap moisture and bacteria. Chronic ear infections are very common in Beagles. Weekly ear cleaning essential.</div></div>
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Intervertebral Disc Disease</div><div class="hi-cost">$3,000 - $6,000</div><div class="hi-desc">Less common than in Dachshunds but Beagles can develop IVDD. Keep weight controlled to reduce spinal stress.</div></div>
          <div class="health-item"><div class="hi-name">Obesity</div><div class="hi-cost">$300 - $1,000/yr</div><div class="hi-desc">Beagles are food-obsessed and will overeat if given the chance. Obesity leads to joint problems and diabetes. Measure every meal strictly.</div></div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:12px;color:#857E77;line-height:1.6">Emergency fund: $3,000 minimum. Beagles are generally healthy but their adventurous nature means surprise vet visits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-wrap">
        <h2>Beagle cost questions</h2>
        <div class="faq"><h3>How much does a Beagle cost to buy?</h3><p>Beagles cost $500-$1,500 from reputable breeders in 2025. Show-quality Beagles can cost up to $2,500. Beagles are widely available from rescue organizations for $50-$300 — they're frequently surrendered by owners who underestimate their howling and escape artist tendencies.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>How much does a Beagle cost per year?</h3><p>Beagles are one of the most affordable breeds at $1,200-$2,000/year in routine years. First year costs run $2,500-$3,500. They're not a premium breed but their nose gets them into expensive situations — budget for one surprise vet visit per year on average.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>Do Beagles need a fenced yard?</h3><p>Yes — a securely fenced yard is essentially required for Beagle ownership. They will follow a scent right out of an unfenced yard and ignore recall commands completely once on a trail. A 6-foot fence costs $2,000-$5,000 to install. Underground fences do not work reliably for scent hounds.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>Is pet insurance worth it for a Beagle?</h3><p>Yes — especially given their tendency to eat foreign objects and their epilepsy risk. Insurance runs $35-$60/month for a Beagle and covers the $1,500-$4,000 intestinal obstruction surgeries that happen when they eat something they shouldn't. A good investment for this adventurous breed.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>What is the lifetime cost of a Beagle?</h3><p>Expect $15,000-$22,000 over a 13-15 year lifespan. Beagles are one of the most affordable breeds to own long-term. The biggest variable is whether they experience a major health event or stay healthy throughout their life.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
